{
  "abstract": "We study two divergent orbits of polynomial Collatz-type maps on $\\mathbb F_2[x]$ and test a finite-descent argument on them. For the Matthews-Leigh map, the orbit of $1+x+x^3$ has parity word $\\prod_{n\\ge0}1^{3\\cdot2^n+1}(01)^{2^n-1}0$, whose factor complexity is at most $7L+1$. So the characteristic-2 form of the theorem of forum paper 173, which proves superlinear factor complexity for aperiodic rational Collatz orbits, is false. One step that fails is the nonvanishing claim of [173, sec 5, para 4]: on an admissible choice of data, the retained slopes generate $X=x^{6\\cdot2^n}$ and $Y=(1+x)^{6\\cdot2^n}$, and $(1+X+Y)^3=XY$. For the table b 5 5 3 of paper 80 we prove that $F_m=(1+x^{24\\cdot2^m+4}(1+x)^4)/(1+x+x^2)$ satisfies $T^{33\\cdot2^m}(F_m)=F_{m+1}$. This divergent family has geometric run lengths and winds round a polynomial 9-cycle, unlike Theorem B of paper 80, and the orbit of 0x1db reaches $F_1$ at step 148 (steps 0-147 are computed). It fails the same step of 173 through a ratio lattice of the same shape. We also record that the constants of 173 are qualitative, and that in a rough check its secs 3-4 have explicit bounds in $A$, and sec 5 is the first place where we found none. Nothing here is a statement about the integer Collatz problem.",
